statement:
For the four transitions $a_{n+1,n},b_{n+1,n},c_{n+1,n},d_{n+1,n}$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
a_{n+1,n}\Theta_{A,n+1}
&=\Theta_{A,n}\Phi_{Y'}(a_{n+1,n}),\\
b_{n+1,n}\Theta_{B,n+1}
&=\Theta_{B,n}\Phi_{Y'}(b_{n+1,n}),\\
c_{n+1,n}\Theta_{C,n+1}
&=\Theta_{C,n}\Phi_{Y'}(c_{n+1,n}),\\
d_{n+1,n}\Theta_{D,n+1}
&=\Theta_{D,n}\Phi_{Y'}(d_{n+1,n}).
\end{aligned}
$$

Hence the four corner Weil structures and all four edges are maps of towers.

proof.text:
Fix $n\geq1$, put $m=n+1$ and $r=r_{m,n}$. Work in the ambient derived categories of $\Lambda$-modules: the reductions $K_i=K\otimes^L_\Lambda P_i$ and $r$ belong to this common category. The comparisons $\kappa_g$ and $\kappa_{g'}$ are isomorphisms of functors, obtained by composing the derived pullback composition isomorphisms for the commuting faces. By the finite-level exchange restriction lemma, the map $\beta_f$ at each reduction is the restriction-of-scalars specialization of the ambient exchange for the Frobenius face, and this identification is natural in coefficient morphisms. Its explicit unit--composition--counit formula is a composite of natural transformations, so its naturality is the defining naturality equation, not projection-formula naturality. Put $F=Lg^*$, $H=Rf_*$, $\kappa_i=\kappa_{g,HK_i}$ and $\beta_i=\beta_{f,K_i}$. Naturality gives $H\Phi_X(r)\beta_m=\beta_n\Phi_Y(Hr)$ and $F\Phi_Y(Hr)\kappa_m=\kappa_n\Phi_{Y'}(FHr)$. Using $b_{m,n}=FHr$ and $r\vartheta_m=\vartheta_n\Phi_X(r)$, compute
$$
\begin{aligned}
b_{m,n}\Theta_{B,m}
&=FH(r\vartheta_m)F(\beta_m)\kappa_m\\
&=FH(\vartheta_n)F(H\Phi_X(r)\beta_m)\kappa_m\\
&=FH(\vartheta_n)F(\beta_n)F\Phi_Y(Hr)\kappa_m\\
&=\Theta_{B,n}\Phi_{Y'}(b_{m,n}).
\end{aligned}
$$
